(MS-DOS version)
Installation instructions for EBaC. (Electronic Bible and Concordance)
Type the following commands (shown indented below) from the C> prompt. 
If you are in Microsoft Windows, you can get a C> prompt by double
clicking on the MS-DOS icon in the Main group of Program Manager.
	c:
	dir
Check to see if you have at least 9,000,000 bytes free.
You must have this much space before you continue the installation.
	cd\
	md bible
	cd bible
	md av
	cd av
Insert one of the disks into your A: drive.
(If your disk drive is called B: then use b: in place of a: below)
	copy a:. .
Insert another disk into your drive.
	copy a:. .
Insert the last disk into your drive.
	copy a:. .
Put the disks away in a safe place.
	copy /b p1 + p2 + p3 bigfile.lzh
	dir bigfile.lzh
Check to see if the size is 2891891. If its not... well it should be.
	del p1
	del p2
	del p3
	lha211
When the program says, "Continue the self-extraction? [Y/N]" press, "y"
These next two commands take a while, so be patient.
	lha e bigfile
	crtolf
	copy trans ..
	del trans
	PATH
Pick a directory from the list shown to put the BIBLE.EXE file into.
Use that location in place of "c:\dos" shown below.
	copy bible.exe c:\dos
	del bible.exe
Now you need to edit your AUTOEXEC.BAT file to insert a few lines.
This next command assumes you have a command called "edit" which
is included with MS-DOS 5.0 and newer. If you need to use a different
editor, that is fine with me.
	edit c:\autoexec.bat
Insert these three lines into the file, and double check your spelling:
   set BIBLEDRIVE=C:
   set BIBLEROOT=\BIBLE
   set TEMPFILE=C:\
If you have installed a RAM disk on your computer which can be accessed
via another drive letter such as E:\ then you can make EBaC
run about twice as fast by setting TEMPFILE to that drive:
   set TEMPFILE=E:\
Don't worry if you don't use a RAM disk. EBaC does not require one.
Now you need to check your CONFIG.SYS file to make sure it
loads the ANSI.SYS driver. It probably does; we just have to make sure.
	type c:\config.sys
Look for a line that says:   DEVICE=C:\DOS\ANSI.SYS
                       or:   DEVICE=C:\ANSI.SYS
If one of these is not there, edit the file and insert the line.
The line must refer to a file that really exists, so use the dir command
to locate where ANSI.SYS really is.
Now, you must reboot the computer before running the program.
To run the program, just type this command: bible


